WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.760
Hey, David, I'm in I'm from Australia and I'm&nbsp;
trying to figure out why so many Americans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.560
do not vote. Isn't it obvious that it is&nbsp;
just the fundamental basic participation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.240
in your democracy and that it is the&nbsp;
starting point for all political involvement?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:20.960
Yes. Yes. I you know, for me, I have said many&nbsp;
times I don't believe in this stuff of if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.360
don't vote, you don't get to complain.&nbsp;
That seems kind of silly and reductive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:32.160
But I do think that anybody who cares&nbsp;
at all about the country they live in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.400
should, as a minimum be voting in addition&nbsp;
to whatever else they are doing. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.000
the question of why more people don't vote is a&nbsp;
good question. It's easy to say, well, more people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.160
don't vote because it's not mandatory. That's&nbsp;
not what what I think is really the interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.440
part. The more interesting part to me is what&nbsp;
are the reasons that people choose not to vote?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.680
And there's a very interesting study of nonvoters,&nbsp;
and you can find this on the 538 website.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:04.960
And it looked at why voters versus nonvoters&nbsp;
end up not voting, but rather it compared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:11.360
the reasons that people who never vote don't vote&nbsp;
versus the reasons that people who sometimes vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:18.160
don't end up voting. One of the reasons that&nbsp;
nonvoters choose never to vote is they believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:23.600
that it doesn't matter who wins. Nothing will&nbsp;
change for people like them and on. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.720
this is one of those kind of fallacious beliefs,&nbsp;
you know, we hear this idea that sometimes people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.760
will say elections really never come down&nbsp;
to one vote. So therefore my vote doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.320
matter. So I'm going to stay home. And while&nbsp;
it's true that elections rarely come down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:45.040
one vote, particularly national elections, if&nbsp;
everybody who had that thought occur to them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:50.960
stayed home or voted, then you actually would be&nbsp;
able to change the outcomes and maybe influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.240
what your day to day life is like. So that's&nbsp;
a really unfortunate one. One of the reasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.760
it feels like things don't change is so many&nbsp;
people aren't voting. If everybody was voting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.920
things would actually change. Another reason that&nbsp;
nonvoters often don't vote is they believe the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.360
system is too broken to be fixed by voting. That's&nbsp;
sort of a version of the same thing. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:17.280
some folks who believe that because of where they&nbsp;
live, their vote doesn't matter, which is a direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:25.520
critique of the Electoral College. And then&nbsp;
lastly, one of the reasons that a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:31.600
nonvoters cite for not voting is they're not sure&nbsp;
if they can vote, which is really just depressing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:38.320
There are situations in which you would not be&nbsp;
allowed to vote because of a criminal conviction.&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.400
And we know that that's being dealt with at the&nbsp;
state level in a variety of different states.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:48.560
But other than not knowing if you are registered&nbsp;
or knowing when you can register, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.280
the idea that people don't vote because&nbsp;
they're not sure if they can vote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.760
that's a failure of the system for sure. And then&nbsp;
you have to look at which party has an interest in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.520
reducing voter turnout. And of course, we&nbsp;
know that it's been the Republican Party.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:05.840
We know that the Republican Party has covertly&nbsp;
been working on reducing turnout for decades and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:12.320
overtly doing it for at least 10 or 12 years.&nbsp;
Check out the Mike Turzai video back in 2012,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:17.840
I believe it is, where he openly says voter ID&nbsp;
is going to deliver Pennsylvania to Mitt Romney.&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:24.000
So that's just a depressing reality. Now, we could&nbsp;
solve a lot of these problems if we eliminate this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.560
idea of voter registration, if it's simply you&nbsp;
turn 18 and you're a citizen, you can vote. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.600
don't need to register. That resolves a lot of&nbsp;
the issue as to whether one is allowed to vote.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:41.680
That should be done. If we deal with this issue&nbsp;
of have I been purged or do I need ID or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.720
all of these things that when we talk about voter,&nbsp;
there's a reason it's called voter suppression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.080
It really does suppress the vote. And then, of&nbsp;
course, people feeling like the system is broken.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:58.320
I mean, to some degree I sympathize with them.&nbsp;
And at the same time, I recognize that the lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:02.160
participation is in elections. The more broken&nbsp;
it's going to be, the more the system will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:08.560
representative of those who choose to vote.&nbsp;
So the voting voting is just the starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.880
point. I get that voting isn't everything,&nbsp;
but it is depressing when I see, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:18.080
fifty two percent turnout. Fortunately, we were&nbsp;
over 60 percent this November. But this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:22.560
real problem in the United States. I don't&nbsp;
know that you really solve it in a deep way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.320
simply by making voting mandatory. My birth&nbsp;
country of Argentina has mandatory voting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:34.720
I don't know that it in itself gives us&nbsp;
less corrupt outcomes, but that's a bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:38.560
issue and probably one for a different day.
We've got a great bonus show for you today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

